<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · other</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brussels Anti-Slavery Conference 1889–1890</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1889–1890</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Conference of 1889–1890 was an international meeting convened at the invitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Its General Act of 2 July 1890 committed the European colonial powers to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and to restrict the trade in firearms and spirits. It also allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> to levy import duties, easing its finances.</p></div>
